No host keys are shipped in this image — they are generated on first boot.

WHY THIS CHANGED
Early images baked in a pre-generated pair because the rootfs was a read-only
squashfs with NO overlay: dropbear's boot-time keygen
(hk_generate_as_needed -> mkdir /etc/dropbear + dropbearkey) had nowhere to
write, so it failed and :22 was refused. Since v6 the overlay is a real,
bounded MTD partition ("rootfs_data"), so /etc/dropbear is writable and the
stock /etc/init.d/dropbear generates ed25519/ecdsa/rsa keys itself if they are
missing or unreadable — which is what should happen.

Baked-in host keys are identical on every unit built from an image, so SSH host
key verification proves nothing about which box you reached, and anyone with
the image has the private keys. Do not put them back.

Consequences worth knowing:
- first boot after a fresh flash takes noticeably longer (RSA keygen on a
  4.4-era MIPS core), and :22 only opens once that finishes; :23 (shd) is up
  immediately either way;
- the keys live in the overlay, so `sysupgrade` keeps them (the overlay is a
  separate partition and is never written) while `sysupgrade -n` or a
  factory reset discards them and the next boot generates a fresh set — your
  SSH client will warn about a changed host key, correctly.

This directory is kept in the image only so that it exists.
